WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:06.660
The 2017 crop of superhero movies is pretty
impressive and it's safe to say that more

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:09.450
than one wildly exceeded expectations.

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:13.099
If you're looking for the best, we're running
down the seven superhero movies released by

00:00:13.099 --> 00:00:17.650
the major studios in 2017, ranked from worst
to best.

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:20.330
Minor spoilers follow.

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:23.680
Justice League
While audiences have been somewhat forgiving

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:28.450
of the DC Extended Universe's growing pains,
critics have been merciless.

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:31.140
Unfortunately, Justice League proved no exception.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:36.600
On the bright side, fans were finally treated
to the visceral thrill of seeing Batman, Superman,

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:41.060
Wonder Woman, Aquaman, Cyborg, and the Flash
together on the big screen.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:45.260
But the film's uneven plotting and forgettable
villain are hard to overlook.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:49.731
Those weaknesses were mitigated to an extent
by a couple of rousing action sequences, and

00:00:49.731 --> 00:00:54.840
the winning presences of Jason Momoa's Aquaman
and Ezra Miller's scene-stealing Flash.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:01:00.090
So, it may not have been the Justice League
all fans wanted, but it was ultimately an

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:04.580
enjoyable, if flawed, vehicle for some of
the most beloved comics characters ever to

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:06.440
grace the page.

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:10.530
The Lego Batman Movie
Fans were delighted when The Lego Batman Movie,

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:15.710
a spinoff of 2014's surprisingly excellent
animated feature The Lego Movie, was announced.

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:20.260
The film continued the emphasis on clever,
quirky, layered humor, and rewarded fans of

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:24.680
the Dark Knight with a steady stream of references
to his past cinematic incarnations.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:29.460
As good as it was, however, The Lego Batman
Movie is still largely inessential.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:34.220
It broke no new ground in terms of superhero
films or Batman stories, failing to hit the

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:38.800
creative highs that the year's superior superhero
movies all reached in one way or another.

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:43.460
It's squarely in the middle of the pack—but
as an animated Lego outing, it's still a heck

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:46.720
of a lot better than the disappointing Lego
Ninjago Movie.

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:48.320
Logan

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:52.040
Hugh Jackman's stunning 17-year run as Wolverine

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:56.760
in the X-Men series may never be surpassed,
and he could hardly have gone out on a higher

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:57.740
note than Logan.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:02.600
The film hit the right notes for the character
in a way that no previous X-Men movie truly

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:07.860
had, thanks to its grim storyline and hard
R rating, which finally allowed for an appropriately

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.120
Wolverine-esque level of violence.

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:13.680
If there was one consistent complaint about
the film, it's that it may have taken its

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:16.420
grim and melancholy tone a bit too far.

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:21.180
The bleak and unforgiving world is consistently
rendered in stark, dusty tones, and the fates

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:23.520
of our major characters aren't pretty.

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:28.120
Logan is a great Wolverine movie at long last,
but this year's best superhero movies were

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:32.100
able to tell stories every bit as compelling—and
not quite as bleak.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:34.320
Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:38.140
2014's Guardians of the Galaxy certainly overperformed.

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:42.720
Nobody expected a team of virtually unknown
characters featuring a  tree and a

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:47.440
gun-toting raccoon to capture the hearts of
moviegoers, but writer/director James Gunn

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:51.400
brought passion and vision—not to mention
a stellar cast—to the project.

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:56.380
Guardians overcame low expectations to become
one of Marvel's most-loved films, meaning

00:02:56.540 --> 00:03:00.000
that its sequel faced expectations that were
anything but low.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:04.620
Fortunately, Gunn and his ragtag team of space
heroes largely delivered with Guardians of

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:06.140
the Galaxy Vol. 2.

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:10.920
The action sequences are even crazier this
time around, but the family-oriented story

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:15.480
also serves as a framework for Gunn to explore
some of the more unlikely family dynamics

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:20.680
of his characters, and to delve into some
of the more complex aspects of their personalities.

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:23.000
"Hahaha.

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:25.700
You just told everyone your deepest darkest
secret."

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:28.100
"No, come on, I think you're overreacting
a little bit."

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:29.780
"You must be so embarrassed!"

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:34.640
Vol. 2 may not quite have lived up to its
predecessor, but it benefited from not trying

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:37.040
too hard to achieve that lofty goal.

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:41.420
Plus: any film that features Kurt Russell
as a quasi-immortal living planet in human

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:44.020
form earns points for sheer awesomeness.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:45.180
Wonder Woman

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:47.760
The DC Extended Universe has largely failed

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:51.440
to live up to expectations, but there's been
one brilliant exception.

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:56.360
Director Patty Jenkins' Wonder Woman, starring
Gal Gadot as the legendary Amazonian, blew

00:03:56.360 --> 00:04:01.780
away audiences, single-handedly resurrecting
hope for the DCEU as a whole.

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:05.600
Jenkins knocked all kinds of holes in the
well-worn theory that audiences wouldn't respond

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:10.980
to a female-centric superhero movie by turning
in a spectacularly shot and choreographed,

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:16.060
well-paced, and flat-out entertaining feature,
anchored by Gadot's alternately graceful and

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:17.740
badass performance.

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:23.100
Wonder Woman suffers from only minor imperfections:
plot-wise, it bears more than a passing resemblance

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:27.660
to Marvel's Captain America: The First Avenger,
and some critics felt Chris Pine's Steve Trevor

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:31.340
could have been de-emphasized in terms of
the title character's motivation.

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:35.800
But overall, Wonder Woman is a spectacular
big-screen solo debut for a character Hollywood

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:37.700
has famously struggled to adapt.

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:42.100
Jenkins' deft directorial touch and refusal
to objectify her lead only served to make

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:46.560
the character's subsequent appearance in Justice
League function as a reminder of this far

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:48.420
superior film.

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:50.380
Spider-Man: Homecoming

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:56.060
Until 2015, Sony Pictures held the sole film

00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:57.160
rights to Spider-Man.

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:01.580
The first appearance of Spidey in the MCU—in
2016's Captain America: Civil War—proved

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:06.420
what audiences have long known: Spider-Man
belongs in a larger universe with the rest

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:06.920
of Marvel's heroes.

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:08.500
"Underoos!"

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:13.600
"Hey everyone!"

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:18.800
It doesn't hurt that Tom Holland is terrific
in the role of Peter Parker, or Michael Keaton—in

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:23.940
a casting masterstroke—creates one of the
more compelling villains in the MCU as Adrian

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:25.860
Toomes/Vulture.

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:30.320
But the true genius of Homecoming is in the
decision to give us the bumbling, insecure,

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:34.820
15-year old Peter that previous cinematic
incarnations never showed, and to let interactions

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:39.060
with classmates and other heroes inform and
develop the character as they've always done

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:40.000
in the comics.

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:45.040
This Spider-Man has plenty of room to grow,
and his first solo vehicle is an outstanding

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:45.680
beginning.

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:47.560
Thor:Ragnarok

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:51.520
Thor: Ragnarok
The best superhero film of 2017 had the lowest

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:52.480
expectations of them all.

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:57.420
Thor: Ragnarok seemed like almost an afterthought—except
to fans who'd been paying attention.

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:01.980
The signing of indie director Taika Waititi
piqued the interest of those familiar with

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:03.380
his unique comic voice.

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:06.240
"When you're a vampire, you become very sexy"

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:09.860
And the news that the plot would incorporate

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:14.400
elements of the Planet Hulk storyline from
the comics—as well as intriguing cast additions

00:06:14.420 --> 00:06:19.680
including Cate Blanchett and Jeff Goldblum—clued
them in that they might be in for something

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:20.240
special.

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:24.260
Thor: Ragnarok destroyed critical and box
office expectations by finding the innate

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:26.960
silliness in the MCU's God of Thunder.

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:28.660
"Revengers'

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:29.660
"Revengers?"

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:32.960
"We don't have to have a name, we can have
no name."

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:38.420
The movie's nonstop humor is liberally balanced
with plenty of stunning action, a brilliant

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:41.960
soundtrack, and a truly threatening villain
in Blanchett's Hela.

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:47.260
The welcome returns of Tom Hiddleston's Loki
and Mark Ruffalo's Bruce Banner/Hulk—not

00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:51.880
to mention some strongly drawn new characters
and one well-placed Doctor Strange cameo—connect

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:58.240
the film to the larger MCU, but Thor: Ragnarok
is its own singularly insane flick—and one

00:06:58.240 --> 00:06:59.040
of Marvel's best.

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:03.340
Here's hoping we get a Waititi-directed Thor
4 as soon as possible.

